# Line endings: decided HERE, not by each clone's configuration.
#
# Why this file exists. Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default, so a clone
# there rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout - including the shell scripts, which then fail
# under Git Bash/MSYS with a syntax error on the first line that mentions no line ending at all.
# The opposite accident is just as easy: an editor on Windows saves CRLF and, with autocrlf=false,
# those bytes are committed. Both happened in this project (two .sh files in job/ arrived CRLF and
# could never run on Linux).
#
# .gitattributes OVERRIDES core.autocrlf and travels with the repository, so the rules below hold
# for every clone on every platform, whatever the local git is configured to do.
#
# The repository is LF-only today (362 tracked files, all i/lf w/lf on 12 Aug 2026), so adding this
# normalises nothing and moves nothing: it only stops the state from drifting.

# Default: git decides what is text; text is stored LF and checked out LF EVERYWHERE.
# Not "eol=native" - a checkout that differs per platform is what makes a .sh unrunnable.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- sources and documents: LF, explicitly ------------------------------------------------------
*.pas    text eol=lf
*.lpr    text eol=lf
*.inc    text eol=lf
*.lpi    text eol=lf
*.bas    text eol=lf
*.wbas   text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.lua    text eol=lf
*.html   text eol=lf
*.ini    text eol=lf
*.cfg    text eol=lf
*.template text eol=lf

# --- scripts ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .sh MUST be LF: on Windows these run under Git Bash/MSYS, and a CR at the end of a line becomes
# part of the last token ("do\r", "{\r"), which reads like a syntax error in the script's own text.
*.sh     text eol=lf
# PowerShell reads LF-only files on both 5.1 and 7, so .ps1 stays LF like everything else and the
# two build scripts remain byte-comparable with each other.
*.ps1    text eol=lf
# cmd.exe is the exception that genuinely needs CRLF: an LF-only batch file can mis-parse labels
# and GOTO. None are tracked today; the rule is here so the first one added is right.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf

# --- never touched: bytes are content, not text -------------------------------------------------
*.wasm   binary
*.basc   binary
*.o      binary
*.ppu    binary
*.a      binary
*.exe    binary
*.dll    binary
*.so     binary
*.dylib  binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.sid    binary
*.wav    binary
